.artifacts/cache/
.artifacts/dispatch/
.artifacts/discoveries/
.artifacts/insights/
.artifacts/pauses/
.artifacts/runs/
# NOTE: no `.artifacts/memory/` rule. `memory/` is RETIRED (v6.4.4) — see
# `skills/context/references/naming-conventions.md §One knowledge silo`.
# Ignoring it is what made it a knowledge black hole: operators put durable
# notes in a directory named "memory" and git silently dropped them.
# `shepherd migrate --layout v4` drains it; `shepherd lint` FAILs if it
# reappears. Leaving it un-ignored means a stray `memory/` shows up in
# `git status` instead of vanishing.

# shepherd context registry + hook runtime state (.shepherd/ namespace — default)
.shepherd/shepherd.db
.shepherd/shepherd.db-journal
.shepherd/shepherd.db-wal
.shepherd/shepherd.db-shm
# legacy DB name retained — do not remove:
.shepherd/root.db
.shepherd/root.db-*
.shepherd/shepherd.lock
.shepherd/project.json
.shepherd/tmp/
.shepherd/logs/
.shepherd/cache/
.shepherd/dispatch/
.shepherd/discoveries/
.shepherd/insights/
.shepherd/pauses/
# NOTE: no `.shepherd/memory/` rule — see the `.artifacts/memory/` note above.
# runs/ split (v6.4.1 artifact schema): durable run artifacts tracked,
# disposable run STATE (run.json, graph/, dispatch/, locks) ignored.
#
# `reports/` and `audits/` moved from the ignored side to the tracked side in
# v6.4.5 (DF-61). They were classified as disposable run state, but they are
# where every expensive dispatch lands its output, and SKILL.md §Principles
# makes that the opposite of disposable: "every top-tier dispatch MUST
# terminate in exactly one durable artifact ... reasoning that lives only in a
# transcript is spend without impact." A discovery report that fetched 8
# primary sources, or an adversarial audit that found a defect by mutation
# testing, is the durable artifact — not scratch.
#
# The rule was also already being violated in practice, which is what made it
# harmful rather than merely wrong: 23 report files and 3 audit files were
# tracked (gitignore does not apply to already-tracked paths), while 9 newer
# reports from the same run were silently untracked. Whether an artifact
# survived depended on whether someone had once run `git add -f`. An allowlist
# that does not match practice reads as "reports are tracked" at a glance and
# drops the next one.
.shepherd/runs/**
!.shepherd/runs/*/
!.shepherd/runs/*/seed.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/mesh.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/plan.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/phase0.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/close.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/handoff.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/dogfood.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/lanes/
!.shepherd/runs/*/lanes/*/
!.shepherd/runs/*/lanes/*/plan.md
# durable dispatch output — see the DF-61 note above
!.shepherd/runs/*/reports/
!.shepherd/runs/*/reports/*.md
!.shepherd/runs/*/audits/
!.shepherd/runs/*/audits/*.md

